From b3e05209d3721a5bd7f1924a2c5939169a1b4959 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 00:19:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] desktop: reconcile late completions and refuse
 unverified retries

Problem: a no-reply stamp can survive an actual late reply, and an
unapplied store refresh can offer to run a prompt twice.
Why 1: completion spread the stamped message's error fields, or appended
another bubble when the settled stream ID was absent (two RED hook tests).
Why 2: store-recovery exhaustion treated four failed reads as proof that
no answer existed (RED watchdog exhaustion regression).
Root (happened): absence of a local answer was treated as authoritative.
Root (not caught): earlier coverage exercised refresh/output deltas but
not completion-only replies or unreadable-store exhaustion.
Recurrence: extends the three supplied no-reply commits; no server change.

Clear only the synthetic notice when a completion carries reply/error
content; keep empty closing frames and unrelated real failures intact.
Stop the bounded store recovery without a retry card if no read applied.
Extract recovery/settlement into a topical helper to respect existing
session-states size and callback-complexity caps.

Proof: late-completion tests fail before the fix and pass after; exhaustion
regression fails against supplied v3 and passes after. Final focused run:
7 files / 129 tests pass. Before extraction/exhaustion changes, related
247 files / 2814 tests pass. Desktop eslint and tsc pass; scripts/check
--base origin/main passes. Added 95s virtual-clock watchdog tests for both
producer wire shapes plus session_key fallback and errorSurface carriage.

Historical cause boundary: no Desktop silent-turn logs were retained, so
it is NOT established whether the original ended verdict was during the
95s throttle hold or at true completion with a dropped transcript read.
MacBook live throttle/screenshot proof remains with the originating Mac
session after deployment; not reproduced by this commit's unit tests.
